// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row vec_mutref_elem_push_store_rehomed_refused (tier 3).
// LEGAL PROGRAM REFUSED: `Vec<&mut i64>` HOLDING `&mut x` AND `&mut y`, WRITTEN THROUGH, DEAD; THEN `x = x + 1`.
//
// Legal Rust: `v` is dead after the block. Legality MEASURED with rustc 1.98.1 (foot of this file). TODAY refused, build 0230e503bd682184 43: "cannot use 'y'
// while it is mutably borrowed" — the second push's loan on `y` is held by `x` through the A2 edge `v -> x`.
// MEASURED: compiles and runs exit 0 under a2elem and a2skipall; still refused under a2elemshr.
// NEIGHBOUR ROWED AT THE STOREEDGE LANDING (2026-09-14m-storeedge-land), REASON 3 — its own cost is measured non-zero: the only arm
// that closes it is the element test (operand type == a type argument of the out-param), and on the landing's build that test
// UN-REFUSES an illegal program the base refuses (hand s18: a generic `W<U>` whose field stores `&mut Vec<&C>`, written through
// `t.x.push(&c)` before `c.bump()`), and adds the false "cannot return reference to local variable 'result'" line to fail fixture
// issue-62007-assign-differing-fields--t22. The landing (se_nomut: no A2 edge unless the operand holds a `&mut`) leaves it refused.
// Member, same measurements: s36 (`Vec<&mut i64>` block push, source used after the Vec dies).
// Found: hand v01, round 2026-09-14l-storeedge.
package vec_mutref_elem_push_store_rehomed_refused;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut x: i64 = 1i64;
    let mut y: i64 = 2i64;
    {
        let mut v: Vec<&mut i64> = Vec::new();
        v.push(&mut x);
        v.push(&mut y);
        *v[0u64] = 7i64;
        *v[1u64] = 8i64;
    }
    x = x + 1i64;
    return (x + y) as i32 - 16i32;
}
